Restore upstream's ULONG_MAX guard so timeout=-1 actually means "no limit".

pam_fprintd(8) documents a negative timeout as "no limit at all", implemented
by setting verification_end = ULONG_MAX (pam_fprintd.c:464) and skipping the
deadline arithmetic. This fork, branched from fprintd ~1.94.2 and unmaintained
since 2022, dropped the guard and unconditionally computes

    wait_time = verification_end - now ();

into an int64_t. With the ULONG_MAX sentinel that subtraction overflows to a
negative value, so `wait_time <= 0` breaks the poll loop on the first
iteration. The subsequent `now () >= verification_end` test is then false --
nothing exceeds ULONG_MAX -- so timed_out is never set and data->result is
still NULL, dropping control to the final else branch, which reports
"An unknown error occurred" instead of ever reading the sensor.

The hunk below is upstream's fix (fprintd pam/pam_fprintd.c:519-525),
minus the __linux__ ifdefs and keeping this fork's stop_got_pw term so the
grosshack password-prompt thread can still cut the wait short.

On the infinite path wait_time stays INT64_MAX and poll()'s int timeout
argument truncates to a negative value, which means "block indefinitely" --
the same behaviour upstream relies on.

--- a/pam/pam_fprintd.c
+++ b/pam/pam_fprintd.c
@@ -497,9 +497,11 @@
       for (;;)
         {
           struct signalfd_siginfo siginfo;
-          int64_t wait_time;
+          int64_t wait_time = INT64_MAX;
+
+          if (verification_end != ULONG_MAX)
+            wait_time = verification_end - now ();
 
-          wait_time = verification_end - now ();
           if (wait_time <= 0 || data->stop_got_pw)
             break;
 
